  "level": "L2",
  "title": "SWA offload masks and fused draft wrappers retain asymmetric ownership",
  "mechanism": "SWAKVPool owns layer-transfer waiting and clears the inner pools' counters to avoid duplicate waits. Its CPU-copy path saves only SWA indices greater than zero, preserving a mask because zero is a dummy slot and tail-only allocations leave some full indices unmapped. Restore intersects that saved selection with the new mapping and skips an empty resulting SWA set. UnifiedDraftSWAKVPool routes full-to-SWA translation through the host allocator; its overridden key/value/KV getters do not wait, but the inherited scale-buffer getter still calls _wait_for_layer. Therefore storing a transfer counter can matter on the scale path. bind_fused_draft deduplicates families in placement.hosts order and applies binders in that order, despite the docstring saying registry order.",
  "corrections": [
    "The draft wrapper's counter is not universally ineffective: inherited scale access still waits.",
    "CPU restore uses old and new mapping membership, rather than blindly replaying saved SWA rows.",
    "Use actual host-placement order rather than the binder-order docstring; no runtime offload or compaction test was performed."
  ],
  "source_scope": "Manually read the listed exact Git ranges for the final corrected mechanism. All other model claims remain research notes; no runtime experiment.",
